#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0488d7ff-b23d-57ca-ac1f-3b003b1d4cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278628} {:answer/id #uuid "19abce14-7fbc-5539-b049-f6588065cc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} {:answer/id #uuid "1b20a70e-e52e-59a8-a425-efc1714f3b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278843} {:answer/id #uuid "4be49a5e-04bf-5a10-ba8f-0ff99825ca37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279271} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c391e8-39ad-5fb2-9766-ec1eb8cec68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19abce14-7fbc-5539-b049-f6588065cc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} :answer-field/id #uuid "11825cc6-3e2c-51ee-8c64-1a71cc1b22e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7670cca-4082-532e-925a-af41e094c0c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163122" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08639b07-25fa-583e-a83f-d337dffebecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281761} {:answer/id #uuid "152ed58b-832e-5b30-8ecd-f7c2f04c36a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281887} {:answer/id #uuid "4577ccab-91a5-5a0a-ad8e-553e88e2a326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282277} {:answer/id #uuid "84d9d89d-783c-5c61-8e59-033da0638e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282835} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6113b6-87b4-5fbe-ade6-6692083e0867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4577ccab-91a5-5a0a-ad8e-553e88e2a326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282277} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23126f6-69b8-59e0-8873-787033890983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0ff3e7e-07e5-5c92-aabb-370f248ce704" :question/math-academy-id "q-164611" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & ≈3+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(4) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600bf2e3-6ccc-5e19-9c6d-a11e065651ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282503} {:answer/id #uuid "88308563-47ab-55ac-b349-b96b91b0c069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282861} {:answer/id #uuid "90dcefcb-039e-53fd-ad50-070bdaeaeafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282943} {:answer/id #uuid "e899ed7d-bdce-5f3e-8857-e9c5204e70b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283714} {:answer/id #uuid "f88eee42-846c-5f36-8d70-74d6ce1e4b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90dcefcb-039e-53fd-ad50-070bdaeaeafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282943} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bee6918-a74e-5833-a9b9-7d8fc82fe9ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f42addea-156e-5dc2-b6d9-50bdb56ae67d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164827" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & ≈2+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d94fce-7e2e-529e-aab4-3e18c1af3fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367910} {:answer/id #uuid "6e57b760-bf40-5678-b21d-6ff872c9be26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367911} {:answer/id #uuid "9902fd92-6ee4-5fc6-9f8c-37e50568e03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367912} {:answer/id #uuid "2e00e804-744b-5915-8e9b-7dead65cc518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367913} {:answer/id #uuid "2bddaab1-a705-5a6e-be30-de431dbdc849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9902fd92-6ee4-5fc6-9f8c-37e50568e03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367912} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0b0d95-711d-5039-9f91-5c80f081aeb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2bdcbd2-3eb2-5c14-bb14-2f06b27bbd7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164862" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =1+e, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(2,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =1+e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+e+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,2) & ≈1+e+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1+e+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1+e+6+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =7+e+\\frac{1}{2}(14) \\\\  & =14+e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
